The Track B audit also fails: the only compact paper-native construction is the nested-triangle lower-bound family from Section 1.1. Once its layers are exposed, a one-pass “choose any adjacent vertex in the next triangle” attack succeeds; once the layers are hidden, recovering them is itself a linear graph decomposition, so the purported shortcut is the same order as `BetterGreedy`. I’m documenting this as an H rejection rather than shipping a planted family with unsupported hardness.
